 tree, which would destroy latency.\"]\n./public/chp/blog/datomworld-vs-rama.blog:72:          [:p \"In summary, colocation is handled by edge caching and mobile continuations, fanout is bypassed by edge-cached pull queries over single-writer streams, and Rama's live distributed data structures address the same need as Datom.world's published, content-addressed index trees, albeit through a very different consistency and update model. Whether deployed as a trustless P2P DHT or backed by S3/Kafka in a centralized data center, Datom.world's logical abstractions remain identical.\"]\n./test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc:163:          address (jing/segment-key payload)\n./test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc:164:          nil-address (jing/materialize! (:local c) nil)]\n./test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc:267:          address (jing/materialize! a v)]\n./test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc:268:      (is (= (jing/segment-key v) address))\n./test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc:277:          address (jing/segment-key {:bytes [4]})]\n./test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc:288:          k (jing/segment-key {:x 1})]\n./test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc:291:      (is (throws? #((:put-content-fn a) :segment/sha256-short {:x 1})))\n./test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc:311:            k (jing/segment-key {:x 1})]\n./test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc:326:          address (jing/segment-key payload)]\n./test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc:345:          nil-address (jing/materialize! (:local b) nil)]\n./test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc:346:      (is (= :none (jing/get a (jing/segment-key {:ghost 1}) :none)))\n./test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc:347:      (is (= (jing/segment-key nil) nil-address))\n./test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc:358:          address (jing/materialize! (:local b) v)]\n./test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc:371:          k (jing/segment-key {:bytes [1]})\n./test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc:402:          address (jing/segment-key payload)\n./test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc:433:          address (jing/segment-key {:x 1})]\n./test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc:497:          address (jing/segment-key payload)]\n./test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc:516:          address (jing/segment-key v)\n./test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc:528:      (jing/materialize! (:local farther-peer) v)\n./public/chp/blog/dao.space-vs-xtdb.blog:48:           [:li [:strong \"Storage (dao.jing):\"] \" A completely dumb, content-addressed store (CAS). \" [:code \"dao.jing\"] \" materializes opaque binary or EDN payloads under strict cryptographic content addresses (\" [:code \":segment/sha256-<hash>\"] \"). It knows nothing about datoms, transactions, entities, or schemas. It does not match, does not query, and exposes no mutable roots.\"]\n./public/chp/blog/dao.space-vs-xtdb.blog:230:           [:li [:strong \"Pure Content-Addressed Storage and P2P Distribution:\"] \" XTDB's storage layout relies on sequential log offsets, central partitions, and mutable checkpoint roots. DaoSpace relies on \" [:code \"dao.jing\"] \", where every segment is addressed strictly by cryptographic hash (\" [:code \":segment/sha256-<hash>\"] \"). This enables DaoSpace to replicate across local files, cloud object stores, or peer-to-peer Distributed Hash Tables (DHTs) with zero cache invalidation and automatic deduplication.\"]\n./test/yin/vm_test.cljc:503:                 (let [id (jing/segment-key (subvec row 1))]\n./test/yin/vm_test.cljc:522:             (error-rule #(vm/semantic-bytecode->ast (assoc bc :root :segment/sha256-0))))))\n./test/yin/vm_test.cljc:537:  (let [id (jing/segment-key body)]\n./test/dao/stream/transit_test.cljc:70:         (is (= (jing/content-hash '(2 3))\n./test/dao/stream/transit_test.cljc:71:                (jing/content-hash (second decoded))))\n./test/dao/stream/transit_test.cljc:73:                (jing/content-hash decoded)))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
