m/ledger.cljc:79:   [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/record (jing/segment-key record)]])\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ledger.cljc:89:   vector's `(jing/segment-key vector)`.\"\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ledger.cljc:91:  (let [segment (jing/segment-key vector)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ledger.cljc:96:     :record-address (jing/segment-key record)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ledger.cljc:187:                       (jing/segment-key (subvec (get rows id) 1)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ledger.cljc:195:           (when-not (= output (jing/segment-key vector))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ledger.cljc:203:               actual (jing/segment-key\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/documentation_index.md:20:- The five state fields (`:control`, `:environment`, `:store`, `:continuation`, `:value`)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:483:   :yin.code/hash {},      ; string — reserved content address\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:793:;; distinct node; `id` is `(jing/segment-key [tag & slots])`.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:938:               id (jing/segment-key body)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:1248:                 _ (when-not (= id (first row) (jing/segment-key body))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/completion.cljc:13:   address is a content address (`dao.jing/segment-key`); a context is the\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/completion.cljc:78:   c}`. A closure or continuation over an unaddressed segment contributes\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/completion.cljc:166:  (let [address (jing/segment-key v)]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/completion.cljc:194:   (let [address (jing/segment-key v)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/completion.cljc:292:    (when (and (:address image) (= (:address image) (jing/segment-key v)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/summary.md:89: :continuation <cont-frame>}       ; Where to return\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:20: :continuation <continuation-or-nil> ; Where to return after evaluation\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:42: :continuation nil\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:49: :continuation nil\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:111:### `:continuation` - Return Context\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:132:{:continuation nil}\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:135:{:continuation {:frame {:type :application\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:142:{:continuation {:frame {...}\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:184: :continuation nil            ; No outer continuation\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:196: :continuation {:frame {:type :application ...} :type :eval-operator}\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:203: :continuation {:frame {...} :type :eval-operand}\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:214: :continuation nil            ; No pending returns\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:276: :continuation nil}\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:282: :continuation {:frame <application-node> :type :eval-operator}}\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:288: :continuation nil}\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:294: :continuation {:frame <application-node> :type :eval-operand}}\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:302: :continuation nil}\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:313:       (nil? (:continuation state))))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:322:(:continuation state)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:335:  (println \"Continuation:\" (:type (:continuation state)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:341:  (when (or (:control state) (:continuation state))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:378:| **K**ontinuation | `:continuation` | Return context |\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:399:{:control ast :environment env}  ; Missing :store, :continuation, :value\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:424:     (nil? (:continuation state)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:468:    (if (or (:control state) (:continuation state))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.md | sed -n '390,535p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/completion.cljc | sed -n '1,230p'; nl -ba src/cljc/datomworld/demo/continuation_transport.cljc | sed -n '1,190p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc | sed -n '430,620p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.md | sed -n '390,535p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/completion.cljc | sed -n '1,230p'; nl -ba src/cljc/datomworld/demo/continuation_transport.cljc | sed -n '1,190p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc | sed -n '430,620p'\"","aggregated_output":"   390\t  handle is never rebound; reattachment is the caller's, by opening a new\n   391\t  coordinate. Diagnostics have no outlet under a blocking driver and are\n   392\t  drained and dropped at every step. Server `stop!` detaches every\n   393\t  session, releases the listener, and is idempotent; handlers run in the\n   394\t  server's single driver thread, so a handler that never returns stalls\n   395\t  every session.\n   396\t- `dao.jing.dht/create-content-dht` and\n   397\t  `dao.jing.dht.node/create-content-dht-udp` — the distributed backend over an\n   398\t  `IDhtNet` transport; see `docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md`.\n   399\t\n   400\t**The observer is implemented.** `observer-state` and `observe-step!` provide\n   401\tthe explicit intake-pool walk described in *Cursor tracking and recovery*,\n   402\twith no atoms, globals, registration, or discovery. The source stream never\n   403\tenters an address or a stored value.\n   404\t\n   405\t**Content addressing is implemented, transitionally.** `content-hash` hashes\n   406\tthe order-normalized print of a value; `segment-key` mints\n   407\t`:segment/sha256-<hash>` addresses; `segment-address?` is the strict address\n   408\ttest the backend layer enforces. As recorded in *Canonical encoding*, the\n   409\tencoder is transitional until the pinned canonical byte encoding lands.\n   410\t\n   411\t## Open items and current limitations\n   412\t\n   413\t- **Canonical encoding.** The order-normalized hand-printer encoder must be\n   414\t  replaced by a pinned, cross-platform canonical byte encoding. Until then,\n   415\t  content addresses are portable only between implementations sharing the\n   416\t  exact print rule; when the encoding lands, `content-hash`, `segment-key`,\n   417\t  and every minted address change together. Three residuals of the\n   418\t  transitional encoder are deferred to that landing: scalar (symbol)\n   419\t  metadata is not address-significant; pathological symbols whose print text\n   420\t  mimics another value's print (e.g. `(symbol \"42\")` vs `42`) can collide;\n   421\t  and ambient print-var bindings (`*print-readably*` and similar) still\n   422\t  reach scalar bytes, since `canonical-print` delegates scalars to `pr-str`\n   423\t  — collection structure and order are rendered by `canonical-print`\n   424\t  itself, so only scalar leaves reach the host printer.\n   425\t- **ClojureDart's `list` mints metadata.** On ClojureDart, `(list ...)` and\n   426\t  `(apply list ...)` return a list carrying `cljd.core`'s own reader metadata\n   427\t  (`{:line … :column … :end-line … :end-column … :tag PersistentList}`).\n   428\t  `order-normalize`, `yin.vm`'s semantic-bytecode projection, and the\n   429\t  ClojureDart Transit decoders (`dao.stream.transit.cljd` behind\n   430\t  `dao.stream.ws`'s incoming frames, and the older `dao.stream.transit`)\n   431\t  clear metadata on the lists they mint, so neither normalization nor a list\n   432\t  decoded off the wire fabricates it. Any other Dart code that builds a\n   433\t  payload with `list` still hands `dao.jing` that metadata, and its `:tag`\n   434\t  survives the reader-position strip, so the payload addresses differently\n   435\t  from the equal list built on another host. Each such constructor must clear\n   436\t  it the same way (`(with-meta (apply list xs) nil)`) until the ClojureDart\n   437\t  defect is fixed upstream or the pinned canonical encoding decides the fate\n   438\t  of metadata.\n   439\t- **Byte arrays are hashed by identity, not content.** `dao.jing.md` lists\n   440\t  byte arrays as a supported representation-level type, but the transitional\n   441\t  encoder's scalar branch falls through to `pr-str`, which on the JVM prints\n   442\t  a byte array as an identity-bearing object literal (`#object[\"[B\" 0x...\n   443\t  \"...\"]`); other hosts print their own identity-bearing form. Two\n   444\t  content-equal byte arrays currently mint different addresses. No\n   445\t  current producer emits byte-array payloads, so this is latent; it must be\n   446\t  fixed (a proper byte-array print rule, or promotion into the pinned\n   447\t  canonical byte encoding) before any producer relies on byte-array content\n   448\t  addressing.\n   449\t- **Backends and transports must fail closed on metadata they cannot carry.**\n   450\t  Metadata is now address-significant in the transitional encoder, but no\n   451\t  durable backend or wire codec in the system carries metadata today: the\n   452\t  file backend (`dao/jing/file.cljc`) writes payloads with plain `pr-str`,\n   453\t  and the transit codec (`dao/stream/transit.cljc`) states metadata is not\n   454\t  on the wire and its portable-value check admits metadata-bearing\n   455\t  collections without complaint. A metadata-bearing payload therefore passes\n   456\t  `materialize!`'s put validation, is written with its metadata silently\n   457\t  dropped, and fails loudly — the whole store becomes unopenable — on replay,\n   458\t  because the replayed frame no longer hashes to its claimed address. This is\n   459\t  not reachable today (no producer emits collection metadata yet), and the\n   460\t  failure mode is loud rather than silently corrupting, so it is not a\n   461\t  blocker for the encoder fix itself. It becomes blocking the moment any\n   462\t  producer (the code-as-tuples pipeline's row/metadata-bearing content, once\n   463\t  that work starts emitting metadata-bearing literals) begins emitting\n   464\t  metadata-bearing payloads. Every backend and transport must, before that\n   465\t  point, either carry metadata through or explicitly refuse a payload whose\n   466\t  round trip through its own codec would not hash back to its address.\n   467\t\n   468\t  **Status 2026-09-18 (U10):** the storage half is closed. The file\n   469\t  backend's put now applies exactly this rule — a payload whose\n   470\t  `pr-str`/EDN round trip does not hash back to its address (any\n   471\t  metadata-bearing payload, since `pr-str` drops collection metadata the\n   472\t  address keeps) is refused before a byte is written; the memory backend\n   473\t  carries values verbatim, so metadata round-trips there. The transport\n   474\t  half is answered differently: Jing content never crosses a transport as\n   475\t  a bare payload value. `dao.jing.stream`'s boundary adapter wraps the\n   476\t  canonical bytes (`dao.jing/canonical-bytes`, the exact bytes the address\n   477\t  digests) as a CBOR byte string on the `dao.stream.cbor` profile and as a\n   478\t  named vector of octets on `dao.stream.transit-json`, so no transport's\n   479\t  value domain — metadata-blind or not — re-encodes the content.\n   480\t- **Durable observer checkpoints / long-running runner.** `observer-state`\n   481\t  and `observe-step!` are single-step and in-process. The checkpoint records,\n   482\t  per member, the stream coordinate plus the transport-minted cursor; the\n   483\t  coordinate is operational and never enters an address (serializability TBD in\n   484\t  the contract). A runner that drives the loop over time remains to be built.\n   485\t- **Explicit materialization acknowledgement.** A publisher observes only\n   486\t  that its payloads were appended to an intake stream. The mechanism by which\n   487\t  it observes that those payloads have been materialized must be expressed\n   488\t  explicitly, potentially as a response stream.\n   489\t- **The content write path as an effect stream.** A synchronous handle that\n   490\t  accepts a write and blocks until durable keeps the storage coupling. The\n   491\t  content write path as an effect stream (durability as data, `materialize!`\n   492\t  no longer returning an address synchronously) is deferred to a larger\n   493\t  write-path redesign.\n   494\t- **Garbage collection.** Content reachability and reclamation belong to a\n   495\t  higher-level retention policy; immutable content otherwise accumulates\n   496\t  forever.\n   497\t- **Async hydration.** Readers of remote or async B-tree content use the\n   498\t  hydration adapter (`dao.data.btree.storage/hydration-storage` and\n   499\t  `hydrate!`), but the async variants (`hydrate-async`, `store-tree-async`)\n   500\t  are deferred until an async DaoJing backend exists. See\n   501\t  `docs/design/dao.data.btree.md` §5.4.\n   502\t\n   503\t  The remote half of that deferral is the **stepped client**: a\n   504\t  non-blocking remote handle with the `request-put` / `request-get` /\n   505\t  `request-materialize` / `step` / `abandon` shape — a client the caller\n   506\t  steps on a host that cannot wait. It is owed to the async hydration\n   507\t  work (`docs/design/dao.data.btree.md` §5.4), not to the v1 deletion: it\n   508\t  needs multi-id dispatch and per-materialization records rather than the\n   509\t  blocking driver's per-id step, and it forces a consumer change on\n   510\t  B-tree hydration.\n   511\t\n   512\t  **Status 2026-09-18 (stepped client):** the client itself is built —\n   513\t  `dao.jing.remote.step` wraps one `dao.stream.rpc` client state with\n   514\t  per-materialization records and id routes, `step` advancing in the\n   515\t  recorded fixed order (retry unsent, poll, terminal-abandon, issue\n   516\t  verify reads in put-id order, drain-and-route), the `:present` verify\n   517\t  hop hashing the read-back against the address exactly as\n   518\t  `materialize!` does, one published completion per materialization\n   519\t  carrying its put id, and no payload retained or published anywhere.\n   520\t  What remains owed to `dao.data.btree.md` §5.4 is the consumer side:\n   521\t  an async backend over this client, `hydrate-async`, and\n   522\t  `store-tree-async`.\n   523\t\n   524\t  **Status 2026-09-18 (consumer side):** built.\n   525\t  `dao.jing.remote.async/async-content` is the async backend: it owns one\n   526\t  stepped state as its single step owner, queues requests from any\n   527\t  caller, and drives `step` with a self-rescheduling pump (setTimeout /\n   528\t  Dart `Timer` / the JVM delayed executor, or an injected `:schedule`),\n   529\t  answering each request's callback exactly once with its published\n   530\t  completion. `dao.data.btree.storage/hydrate-async` and\n   531\t  `store-tree-async` consume it through a `hydration-storage` whose\n   532\t  source is that handle; see `dao.data.btree.md` §6 Phase 4 notes.\n   533\t\n   534\t## Lineage\n   535\t\n     1\t(ns yin.vm.completion\n     2\t  \"Dependency completion — the work-item fixed point\n     3\t   (`docs/design/yin.vm.dependency-completion.md`, U14; refines\n     4\t   `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` §7.7.2–7.7.3 and UCF §7.6.1–7.6.3).\n     5\t\n     6\t   For one quiescent `SemanticVM`, `complete` computes the conservative\n     7\t   closure the lift needs: `:yin.k/requires` (every field), the reachable\n     8\t   store slice, the referenced parked slice, and `:yin.k/discovery`. It\n     9\t   encodes nothing, lifts nothing, and checks no satisfaction: it hands\n    10\t   those phases finished sets.\n    11\t\n    12\t   The unit of analysis is the work item `[address context]` (§7.7.3). An\n    13\t   address is a content address (`dao.jing/segment-key`); a context is the\n    14\t   finite abstraction of a captured environment (§4): the set of abstract\n    15\t   values an env reaches, names and collection shape dropped — sound because\n    16\t   an environment never discharges a name (UCF §7.6.1 *Names*) and only\n    17\t   contributes reachable values. Analysis factorizes into code facts (once\n    18\t   per address) and value facts (once per context); both are memoized in\n    19\t   the walk state, which is one persistent map, monotone in every field.\n    20\t\n    21\t   Everything here is a pure function over that state map. The store and\n    22\t   parked slices are pulled, never copied wholesale, and hold raw emitter\n    23\t   values: encoding happens after convergence, by the lift.\"\n    24\t  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\n    25\t            [dao.space.query :as query]\n    26\t            [yin.vm :as vm]\n    27\t            [yin.vm.code :as code]\n    28\t            [yin.vm.module :as module]))\n    29\t\n    30\t\n    31\t;; =============================================================================\n    32\t;; Vocabulary\n    33\t;; =============================================================================\n    34\t\n    35\t(def ffi-pair-keys\n    36\t  \"The FFI pair's store keys (UCF §7.5.4 / §7.6.2): never part of a store\n    37\t   slice — the pair is a receiver capability, named by `:yin.k/ffi-ops`.\"\n    38\t  #{vm/call-in-stream-key vm/call-out-stream-key vm/call-out-cursor-key})\n    39\t\n    40\t\n    41\t(def ^:private layout-profile\n    42\t  \"The one lowering profile whose body layout `segment-scope` reads.\"\n    43\t  \"ast-to-bytecode\")\n    44\t\n    45\t\n    46\t(defn- refuse\n    47\t  \"Append one refusal, once: refusals are a vector for stable reporting and\n    48\t   a set in effect, so re-ruling stays idempotent (§8 step 4).\"\n    49\t  [state refusal]\n    50\t  (if (some #(= refusal %) (:refusals state))\n    51\t    state\n    52\t    (update state :refusals conj refusal)))\n    53\t\n    54\t\n    55\t;; =============================================================================\n    56\t;; §4 The context abstraction\n    57\t;; =============================================================================\n    58\t\n    59\t(declare context-of)\n    60\t\n    61\t\n    62\t(defn- address-of\n    63\t  \"The content address of runtime segment id `seg`, through the loaded\n    64\t   image's `:address`; nil when the image claims none (§3).\"\n    65\t  [vm seg]\n    66\t  (get-in vm [:code seg :address]))\n    67\t\n    68\t\n    69\t(defn- typed?\n    70\t  [v type]\n    71\t  (and (map? v) (= type (:type v))))\n    72\t\n    73\t\n    74\t(defn abstract-value\n    75\t  \"§4 `abstract`, flattened: the set of abstract values `v` contributes.\n    76\t   A scalar contributes none; a collection the union over its elements;\n    77\t   one VM value exactly one. `env` is `{:vm vm :registry r :canonical-names\n    78\t   c}`. A closure or continuation over an unaddressed segment contributes\n    79\t   `[:unaddressed seg]`, which the fold turns into a refusal — there is\n    80\t   nothing portable to name.\"\n    81\t  [env v]\n    82\t  (let [{:keys [vm registry canonical-names]} env\n    83\t        addr (fn [seg] (address-of vm seg))\n    84\t        frame (fn [f]\n    85\t                (if-let [a (addr (:segment f))]\n    86\t                  [:frame a (:pc f) (context-of env (:env f))]\n    87\t                  [:unaddressed (:segment f)]))]\n    88\t    (cond\n    89\t      (typed? v :closure)\n    90\t      (if-let [a (addr (:segment v))]\n    91\t        #{[:closure a (:entry v) (context-of env (:env v))]}\n    92\t        #{[:unaddressed (:segment v)]})\n    93\t\n    94\t      (typed? v :stream-ref) #{[:stream (:id v)]}\n    95\t      (typed? v :cursor-ref) #{[:cursor (:id v)]}\n    96\t      (typed? v :parked-continuation) #{[:parked (:id v)]}\n    97\t\n    98\t      (typed? v :reified-continuation)\n    99\t      (if-let [a (addr (:segment v))]\n   100\t        #{[:k a (:pc v) (context-of env (:env v))\n   101\t           (mapv frame (:k v))\n   102\t           (into #{} (mapcat #(abstract-value env %)) (:stack v))]}\n   103\t        #{[:unaddressed (:segment v)]})\n   104\t\n   105\t      (fn? v)\n   106\t      (let [sym (vm/name-of registry canonical-names v)]\n   107\t        (cond\n   108\t          (nil? sym) #{[:unnamed-function]}\n   109\t          (= ::vm/ambiguous sym) #{[:ambiguous-primitive]}\n   110\t          :else #{[:primitive sym]}))\n   111\t\n   112\t      (map? v) (into #{} (mapcat #(abstract-value env %))\n   113\t                     (concat (keys v) (vals v)))\n   114\t      (coll? v) (into #{} (mapcat #(abstract-value env %)) v)\n   115\t      :else #{})))\n   116\t\n   117\t\n   118\t(defn context-of\n   119\t  \"§4 `context(E)`: the set of abstract values of E's bindings. A plain\n   120\t   persistent set — structural equality is context identity. Well-founded:\n   121\t   env values were created before the env that holds them, so the\n   122\t   env-reachable graph is acyclic; cycles arise only through the store,\n   123\t   which is no part of any context.\"\n   124\t  [env E]\n   125\t  (into #{} (mapcat #(abstract-value env %)) (vals E)))\n   126\t\n   127\t\n   128\t;; =============================================================================\n   129\t;; §5.1 code-facts(address)\n   130\t;; =============================================================================\n   131\t\n   132\t(defn- closure-ranges\n   133\t  \"`[[c body r] …]` for every `:closure` of canonical vector `v`: the body\n   134\t   of the closure at pc `c` is `[body, r]`, `r` the first `:return` at or\n   135\t   after `body` (§5.1.1). nil `r` when there is none.\"\n   136\t  [v]\n   137\t  (let [n (count v)]\n   138\t    (keep (fn [c]\n   139\t            (let [t (nth v c)]\n   140\t              (when (= :closure (nth t 0))\n   141\t                (let [body (nth t 2)]\n   142\t                  [c body (first (filter #(= :return (nth (nth v %) 0))\n   143\t                                         (range body n)))]))))\n   144\t          (range n))))\n   145\t\n   146\t\n   147\t(defn- layout-conforms?\n   148\t  \"Whether `v` has the `\\\"ast-to-bytecode\\\"` body layout the range rule\n   149\t   reads: every body closed by a `:return`, no body covering pc 0 (bodies\n   150\t   follow the main code), and bodies pairwise disjoint (contiguous, never\n   151\t   interleaved). A vector that fails is ruled all-free (§5.1.1).\"\n   152\t  [v]\n   153\t  (let [ranges (closure-ranges v)\n   154\t        spans (distinct (map (fn [[_ b r]] [b r]) ranges))]\n   155\t    (and (every? (fn [[b r]] (and r (pos? b))) spans)\n   156\t         (= (count spans) (count (distinct (map second spans))))\n   157\t         (every? (fn [[[b1 r1] [b2 r2]]]\n   158\t                   (or (< r1 b2) (< r2 b1)))\n   159\t                 (for [x spans, y spans :when (neg? (compare x y))] [x y])))))\n   160\t\n   161\t\n   162\t(defn segment-scope\n   163\t  \"§5.1.1's scope relation `#{[address pc c]}` of one canonical vector: pc\n   164\t   lies in the body of the closure at pc `c`.\"\n   165\t  [v]\n   166\t  (let [address (jing/segment-key v)]\n   167\t    (into #{}\n   168\t          (mapcat (fn [[c body r]]\n   169\t                    (when r\n   170\t                      (map (fn [pc] [address pc c]) (range body (inc r))))))\n   171\t          (closure-ranges v))))\n   172\t\n   173\t\n   174\t(def ^:private segment-scope-rules\n   175\t  '[[(seg-bound? ?addr ?pc ?name)\n   176\t     [$scope ?addr ?pc ?c]\n   177\t     [$code ?addr ?c :closure ?params _]\n   178\t     [(member? ?params ?name)]]\n   179\t    [(seg-bound? ?addr ?pc ?name)\n   180\t     [$scope ?addr ?pc ?c]\n   181\t     (seg-bound? ?addr ?c ?name)]])\n   182\t\n   183\t\n   184\t(defn segment-free-names\n   185\t  \"§5.1.1 segment side: every `:var` name free at some pc of canonical\n   186\t   vector `v`. A `:var` at pc `p` is bound iff `p` lies in the body of a\n   187\t   `:closure` whose params hold the name, transitively through enclosing\n   188\t   bodies. `profile` is the segment's lowering profile; anything but\n   189\t   `\\\"ast-to-bytecode\\\"` — or a vector that does not have that profile's\n   190\t   layout — rules every `:var` free: over-approximating obligations is\n   191\t   always admissible (§7.7.2).\"\n   192\t  ([v] (segment-free-names v layout-profile))\n   193\t  ([v profile]\n   194\t   (let [address (jing/segment-key v)\n   195\t         db (query/relation (code/project-segment-qualified v))]\n   196\t     (if (and (= layout-profile profile) (layout-conforms? v))\n   197\t       (set (map first\n   198\t                 (query/collect\n   199\t                   (query/q '[:find ?name :in $code $scope % ?addr\n   200\t                              :where\n   201\t                              [$code ?addr ?pc :var ?name]\n   202\t                              (not (seg-bound? ?addr ?pc ?name))]\n   203\t                            db\n   204\t                            (query/relation (segment-scope v))\n   205\t                            segment-scope-rules\n   206\t                            address\n   207\t                            {:fns vm/occurrence-fns}))))\n   208\t       (set (map first\n   209\t                 (query/collect\n   210\t                   (query/q '[:find ?name :in $code\n   211\t                              :where [$code _ _ :var ?name]]\n   212\t                            db))))))))\n   213\t\n   214\t\n   215\t(defn- single-column\n   216\t  [q db]\n   217\t  (set (map first (query/collect (query/q q db)))))\n   218\t\n   219\t\n   220\t(defn code-facts\n   221\t  \"§5.1: what one address contributes regardless of context. `payload` is\n   222\t   what `fetch` answered: a canonical instruction vector (or\n   223\t   `{:vector v}`), or a tree `{:root id :rows {id row}}`. `opts`:\n   224\t     :profile  the segment's lowering profile (default \\\"ast-to-bytecode\\\")\n   225\t     :tree     the tree a `:derive` record lowers to this segment, when the\n   226\t               ledger holds one — its `free-names` take precedence (§5.1.1)\n   227\t\n   228\t   `:closures` is per-side diagnostic — entry pcs for a segment, `:lambda`\n   229\t   row ids for a tree — since the context arrives with the value, not the\n   230\t   code; the other five fields are the §7.7.1 conformance surface.\"\n     1\t(ns datomworld.demo.continuation-transport\n     2\t  \"Continuation transport on DaoStream v2, for the v2 continuation demo.\n     3\t\n     4\t   This is `datomworld.continuation-transport` re-expressed on\n     5\t   `dao.stream`. Three v1 idioms do not survive:\n     6\t\n     7\t   - **Cursors are opaque.** v1 seeded `{:position 0}` and read `:position`\n     8\t     back out. v2 mints every cursor at `:dao.stream/oldest` and advances by\n     9\t     the exact successor `next` returns; nothing here inspects a cursor.\n    10\t   - **`append!` takes a batch, not a datom.** One transport event is one\n    11\t     single-element batch, so a reader sees one event per `next`.\n    12\t   - **There is no position to key `:pending-ks` on.** v1 used the stream\n    13\t     position as the correlation id. v2 assigns its own monotone `:seq` and\n    14\t     carries it in the event summary, so correlation is composition bookkeeping\n    15\t     rather than a property of the transport.\n    16\t\n    17\t   The full continuation payload still travels off-stream in `:pending-ks`:\n    18\t   `:make-stream`, `:primitives` and `:bridge` are functions and are\n    19\t   re-supplied by the receiving composition, so this demo does not pretend to\n    20\t   serialize them. Stream capacity is a correctness parameter here, not a\n    21\t   tuning knob — an evicted event is a continuation the receiving VM can never\n    22\t   pick up, so a `gap` is raised rather than retried.\"\n    23\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\n    24\t            [dao.stream.ringbuffer :as ringbuffer]))\n    25\t\n    26\t\n    27\t(def transport-capacity\n    28\t  \"Declared capacity of the continuation medium, in events.\"\n    29\t  4096)\n    30\t\n    31\t\n    32\t(defn make-medium\n    33\t  \"One DaoStream v2 ring buffer for continuation events.\"\n    34\t  []\n    35\t  (ringbuffer/create!\n    36\t    {:dao.stream/type ringbuffer/transport-type,\n    37\t     ringbuffer/capacity-key transport-capacity}))\n    38\t\n    39\t\n    40\t(defn- mint-cursor!\n    41\t  \"Mint the reader cursor this medium starts every consumer at, or throw.\"\n    42\t  [handle what]\n    43\t  (let [result (stream/cursor handle stream/anchor-oldest)\n    44\t        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome result)]\n    45\t    (when-not (= :dao.stream/ok outcome)\n    46\t      (throw (ex-info \"Continuation cursor mint failed\"\n    47\t                      {:what what, :outcome outcome})))\n    48\t    (:dao.stream/cursor result)))\n    49\t\n    50\t\n    51\t(defn init-state\n    52\t  \"Create a fresh transport state. vm-keys is a collection of keywords\n    53\t   identifying the consuming VMs (e.g. [:vm-a :vm-b]).\"\n    54\t  [vm-keys]\n    55\t  (let [k-stream (:dao.stream/handle (make-medium))\n    56\t        cursor (mint-cursor! k-stream :continuation-reader)]\n    57\t    {:k-stream k-stream,\n    58\t     :cursors (into {} (map (fn [k] [k cursor])) vm-keys),\n    59\t     :pending-ks {},\n    60\t     :seq 0}))\n    61\t\n    62\t\n    63\t(defn append-event!\n    64\t  \"Append one datom-shaped event as a single-element batch. Returns the\n    65\t   outcome, so a caller can treat a failed append as what it is.\"\n    66\t  [transport a v seq-n]\n    67\t  (stream/append! transport [[(+ 7000 seq-n) a v (+ 1 seq-n) 0]]))\n    68\t\n    69\t\n    70\t(defn enqueue-k\n    71\t  \"Append a continuation summary to the medium and keep the full k payload\n    72\t   off-stream in :pending-ks, correlated by this composition's own :seq.\"\n    73\t  [state summary k]\n    74\t  (let [seq-n (:seq state)\n    75\t        result (append-event! (:k-stream state)\n    76\t                              :stream/k\n    77\t                              (assoc summary :seq seq-n)\n    78\t                              seq-n)\n    79\t        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome result)]\n    80\t    (when-not (= :dao.stream/ok outcome)\n    81\t      (throw (ex-info \"Continuation event not appended\"\n    82\t                      {:outcome outcome, :seq seq-n})))\n    83\t    (-> state\n    84\t        (assoc :seq (inc seq-n))\n    85\t        (assoc-in [:pending-ks seq-n] k))))\n    86\t\n    87\t\n    88\t(defn enqueue-batch\n    89\t  \"Append a whole continuation in-band: `datoms` followed by the summary\n    90\t   datom a reader addresses by. Nothing is kept off-stream, so the reader\n    91\t   rebuilds the continuation from the batch alone.\"\n    92\t  [state summary datoms]\n    93\t  (let [seq-n (:seq state)\n    94\t        result (stream/append! (:k-stream state)\n    95\t                               (conj (vec datoms)\n    96\t                                     [(+ 7000 seq-n) :stream/k\n    97\t                                      (assoc summary :seq seq-n) (+ 1 seq-n) 0]))\n    98\t        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome result)]\n    99\t    (when-not (= :dao.stream/ok outcome)\n   100\t      (throw (ex-info \"Continuation batch not appended\"\n   101\t                      {:outcome outcome, :seq seq-n})))\n   102\t    (assoc state :seq (inc seq-n))))\n   103\t\n   104\t\n   105\t(defn- fail-read\n   106\t  [state vm-key outcome]\n   107\t  (throw (ex-info \"Continuation stream read failed\"\n   108\t                  {:vm-key vm-key,\n   109\t                   :outcome outcome,\n   110\t                   :pending (count (:pending-ks state))})))\n   111\t\n   112\t\n   113\t(defn consume-k-for\n   114\t  \"Consume the next continuation addressed to vm-key from the medium.\n   115\t   Returns [state' message-or-nil].\n   116\t\n   117\t   Skips events addressed elsewhere, always advancing past them. When the\n   118\t   medium has nothing further, the cursor reached so far is persisted and nil\n   119\t   is returned — the same \\\"always advances\\\" contract the v1 version kept.\"\n   120\t  [state vm-key]\n   121\t  (loop [state state\n   122\t         cursor (get-in state [:cursors vm-key])]\n   123\t    (let [result (stream/next (:k-stream state) cursor)\n   124\t          outcome (:dao.stream/outcome result)]\n   125\t      (cond\n   126\t        (= :dao.stream/ok outcome)\n   127\t        (let [cursor' (:dao.stream/cursor result)\n   128\t              batch (:dao.stream/value result)\n   129\t              [_e a summary _t _m] (peek batch)]\n   130\t          (if (and (= a :stream/k) (= (:to summary) vm-key))\n   131\t            (let [seq-n (:seq summary)\n   132\t                  k (get-in state [:pending-ks seq-n])]\n   133\t              [(-> state\n   134\t                   (assoc-in [:cursors vm-key] cursor')\n   135\t                   (update :pending-ks dissoc seq-n))\n   136\t               {:from (:from summary),\n   137\t                :to (:to summary),\n   138\t                :summary summary,\n   139\t                :k k,\n   140\t                :batch batch}])\n   141\t            (recur state cursor')))\n   142\t\n   143\t        (= :dao.stream/blocked outcome)\n   144\t        [(assoc-in state [:cursors vm-key] cursor) nil]\n   145\t\n   146\t        :else (fail-read state vm-key outcome)))))\n   147\t\n   148\t\n   149\t(defn read-events-from\n   150\t  \"Drain the events visible from `cursor`, returning [events cursor'].\n   151\t   Used by the composition's views, which read without consuming.\"\n   152\t  [transport cursor]\n   153\t  (loop [cursor cursor\n   154\t         acc []]\n   155\t    (let [result (stream/next transport cursor)\n   156\t          outcome (:dao.stream/outcome result)]\n   157\t      (if-not (= :dao.stream/ok outcome)\n   158\t        [acc cursor]\n   159\t        (recur (:dao.stream/cursor result)\n   160\t               (into acc (:dao.stream/value result)))))))\n   161\t\n   162\t\n   163\t(defn oldest-cursor!\n   164\t  \"Mint a fresh read cursor at the oldest retained event.\"\n   165\t  [transport]\n   166\t  (mint-cursor! transport :transport-view))\n   167\t\n   168\t\n   169\t(defn in-flight-summary\n   170\t  \"Summarize the continuation events visible to each VM between that VM's\n   171\t   cursor and the head of the medium. Reads without consuming.\"\n   172\t  [transport cursors]\n   173\t  (into []\n   174\t        (mapcat\n   175\t          (fn [[vm-key cur]]\n   176\t            (let [[events _] (read-events-from transport cur)]\n   177\t              (keep (fn [[_e a summary _t _m]]\n   178\t                      (when (and (= a :stream/k) (= (:to summary) vm-key))\n   179\t                        {:from (:from summary),\n   180\t                         :to (:to summary),\n   181\t                         :control (:control summary),\n   182\t                         :k-depth (:k-depth summary),\n   183\t                         :seq (:seq summary)}))\n   184\t                    events)))\n   185\t          cursors)))\n   430\t   datoms.\n   431\t\n   432\t   The local stream is on a complete-retention transport\n   433\t   (dao.stream.memory-log), so a fresh :oldest cursor is the origin and\n   434\t   `gap` cannot occur — completeness comes from the transport's declared\n   435\t   retention, never from the anchor (dao.stream.md, *Complete history*).\n   436\t   `blocked` (an open stream caught up) and `end` (a closed one fully read)\n   437\t   finish the read at the tail. Each element is validated and flattened as it\n   438\t   is read, so the first defect in stream order is the one reported and no\n   439\t   read happens past it. A well-formed but unexpected outcome means the handle\n   440\t   is not the transport the composition owes.\"\n   441\t  [local-stream]\n   442\t  (let [mint (checked :cursor (stream/cursor local-stream :dao.stream/oldest))]\n   443\t    (when-not (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome mint))\n   444\t      (throw (ex-info \"local stream refused an :oldest cursor\" {:result mint})))\n   445\t    (loop [cursor (:dao.stream/cursor mint)\n   446\t           datoms []]\n   447\t      (let [r (checked :next (stream/next local-stream cursor))]\n   448\t        (case (:dao.stream/outcome r)\n   449\t          :dao.stream/ok\n   450\t          (recur (:dao.stream/cursor r)\n   451\t                 (into datoms (element-datoms (:dao.stream/value r))))\n   452\t          (:dao.stream/blocked :dao.stream/end) datoms\n   453\t          (throw (ex-info \"local stream is not a complete-retention transport\"\n   454\t                          {:outcome (:dao.stream/outcome r), :result r})))))))\n   455\t\n   456\t\n   457\t(defn- recording-content-handle\n   458\t  \"Temporary in-memory content store for the publish build. :put-content-fn\n   459\t   records each unique node blob on first insertion (answering :present for\n   460\t   duplicates, so the recorded order is first-insertion order, deduplicated);\n   461\t   :get-content-fn reads recorded blobs back. Addresses are minted by\n   462\t   jing/materialize! through dao.data.btree.storage/kv-storage, so the\n   463\t   recorded order is exactly the store-tree children-before-parent traversal.\n   464\t   The handle is a build-time value: no global state is introduced.\"\n   465\t  []\n   466\t  (let [state (atom {:content {}, :order []})]\n   467\t    {:state state,\n   468\t     :put-content-fn (fn [address payload]\n   469\t                       (if (contains? (:content @state) address)\n   470\t                         :present\n   471\t                         (do (swap! state\n   472\t                                    (fn [s]\n   473\t                                      (-> s\n   474\t                                          (assoc-in [:content address] payload)\n   475\t                                          (update :order conj [address payload]))))\n   476\t                             :inserted))),\n   477\t     :get-content-fn (fn [address not-found]\n   478\t                       (get-in @state [:content address] not-found))}))\n   479\t\n   480\t\n   481\t(defn- validate-branching!\n   482\t  [branching]\n   483\t  (when-not (and (integer? branching) (<= 2 branching))\n   484\t    (throw (ex-info\n   485\t             \"publish-index! :branching-factor must be an integer of at least 2\"\n   486\t             {:branching-factor branching})))\n   487\t  branching)\n   488\t\n   489\t\n   490\t(defn- select-intake-stream!\n   491\t  [intake-pool select-fn]\n   492\t  (when-not (coll? intake-pool)\n   493\t    (throw\n   494\t      (ex-info\n   495\t        \"publish-index! intake-pool must be a collection of writable dao.stream values\"\n   496\t        {:intake-pool intake-pool})))\n   497\t  (when (empty? intake-pool)\n   498\t    (throw (ex-info \"publish-index! intake-pool must be non-empty\"\n   499\t                    {:intake-pool intake-pool})))\n   500\t  (when-not (fn? select-fn)\n   501\t    (throw (ex-info\n   502\t             \"publish-index! :select-stream must be a function of the pool\"\n   503\t             {:select-stream select-fn})))\n   504\t  (let [selected (select-fn intake-pool)]\n   505\t    (when-not (boolean (some #(identical? % selected) intake-pool))\n   506\t      (throw\n   507\t        (ex-info\n   508\t          \"publish-index! :select-stream returned a value outside the intake pool\"\n   509\t          {:selected selected, :intake-pool intake-pool})))\n   510\t    selected))\n   511\t\n   512\t\n   513\t(defn- append-ok!\n   514\t  \"Append one opaque payload to an intake stream; every stream/append!\n   515\t   must answer `:dao.stream/ok`, anything else throws with the result\n   516\t   attached.\"\n   517\t  [stream payload]\n   518\t  (let [result (stream/append! stream payload)]\n   519\t    (when-not (and (map? result) (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome result)))\n   520\t      (throw (ex-info (str \"publish-index! stream append failed: \"\n   521\t                           (pr-str result))\n   522\t                      {:stream stream, :result result, :payload payload})))\n   523\t    result))\n   524\t\n   525\t\n   526\t(defn publish-index!\n   527\t  \"The transactor entry point, agent-side (docs/design/dao.jing.md,\n   528\t   Publication from an agent).\n   529\t\n   530\t   1. Snapshots the agent's local stream (snapshot-datoms) — fully, before\n   531\t      any publication append. Atomic transaction records are flattened here.\n   532\t   2. Builds the four covered indexes into a temporary recording content\n   533\t      handle through dao.data.btree.storage/kv-storage, so addresses are\n   534\t      minted by jing/materialize! and equal blobs deduplicate in\n   535\t      children-before-parent first-insertion order.\n   536\t   3. Appends every unique node blob in that recorded order, then the\n   537\t      manifest, all to exactly the intake stream selected from the pool.\n   538\t   4. Returns {:manifest-address (jing/segment-key manifest) :manifest\n   539\t      manifest}.\n   540\t\n   541\t   The manifest address is derived from the manifest alone and never depends\n   542\t   on which intake stream carried it. A partial immutable prefix on a full\n   543\t   intake stream is acceptable and retry-safe: the manifest is always\n   544\t   appended last, so a :full failure can only have left node blobs.\n   545\t\n   546\t   Success acknowledges that every payload was appended to the selected\n   547\t   intake stream. It does not acknowledge that an asynchronous DaoJing\n   548\t   observer has materialized those payloads yet. Because the build starts at\n   549\t   the retained history's origin and reconstructs complete indexes,\n   550\t   local-stream must be on a complete-retention transport\n   551\t   (dao.stream.memory-log); the snapshot is complete before anything is\n   552\t   emitted.\n   553\t\n   554\t   Usage:\n   555\t     (publish-index! local-stream intake-pool)\n   556\t     (publish-index! local-stream intake-pool\n   557\t                     {:branching-factor n, :select-stream f})\n   558\t\n   559\t   opts: {:branching-factor n — max keys per node, at least 2 (default 512)\n   560\t          :select-stream f   — receives the pool and returns the intake\n   561\t                               stream to append to (default first)}\"\n   562\t  ([local-stream intake-pool] (publish-index! local-stream intake-pool nil))\n   563\t  ([local-stream intake-pool opts]\n   564\t   (let [branching (validate-branching! (:branching-factor opts 512))\n   565\t         intake (select-intake-stream! intake-pool (:select-stream opts first))\n   566\t         datoms (snapshot-datoms local-stream)\n   567\t         handle (recording-content-handle)\n   568\t         storage (bts/kv-storage handle {:branching-factor branching})\n   569\t         build-tree\n   570\t         (fn [cmp]\n   571\t           (bt/from-sequential cmp datoms {:branching-factor branching}))\n   572\t         trees {:eavt (build-tree eavt-cmp),\n   573\t                :aevt (build-tree aevt-cmp),\n   574\t                :avet (build-tree avet-cmp),\n   575\t                :vaet (build-tree vaet-cmp)}\n   576\t         root-addr (fn [tree]\n   577\t                     ;; an empty index has no root node; nil is the\n   578\t                     ;; explicit\n   579\t                     ;; \"nothing here\" (walk of nil => ())\n   580\t                     (when (seq datoms) (bt/store-tree tree storage)))\n   581\t         manifest {:indexes {:eavt (root-addr (:eavt trees)),\n   582\t                             :aevt (root-addr (:aevt trees)),\n   583\t                             :avet (root-addr (:avet trees)),\n   584\t                             :vaet (root-addr (:vaet trees))},\n   585\t                   ;; Covered indexes are sets. Their count must describe\n   586\t                   ;; the distinct tuples actually stored, not duplicate\n   587\t                   ;; occurrences in the source stream.\n   588\t                   :count (bt/count (:eavt trees)),\n   589\t                   :branching-factor branching}]\n   590\t     (doseq [[_ payload] (:order @(:state handle))] (append-ok! intake payload))\n   591\t     (append-ok! intake manifest)\n   592\t     {:manifest-address (jing/segment-key manifest), :manifest manifest})))\n   593\t\n   594\t\n   595\t;; =============================================================================\n   596\t;; The index session — dao.space.index as a dao.stream observer\n   597\t;; (docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md)\n   598\t;; =============================================================================\n   599\t\n   600\t;; The index state is the consumer half of one\n   601\t;; dao.stream.observer/run-on-stream session:\n   602\t;;\n   603\t;;   {:indexes  {:eavt bt :aevt bt :avet bt :vaet bt}  ; dao.data.btree values,\n   604\t;;                                                          structurally shared across batches\n   605\t;;    :storage  <recording kv-storage>                ; store-tree target; its identity\n   606\t;;                                                          persists across publishes (§4.1)\n   607\t;;    :recorder <recording content handle>            ; blobs recorded since the last\n   608\t;;                                                          flush; drained at each accepted\n   609\t;;                                                          manifest\n   610\t;;    :branching-factor n                             ; every node the session folds or\n   611\t;;                                                          restores splits at\n   612\t;;    :mode     :resolved | :unresolved               ; fixed at construction (§3.1)\n   613\t;;    :ids      nil | {:next-eid n :ownership k}      ; allocator; :unresolved only\n   614\t;;    :batch    n                                     ; ordinal of the next batch,\n   615\t;;                                                          observer-local\n   616\t;;    :schema   {attr {:db/valueType ...}}            ; supplied; which attrs are refs\n   617\t;;    :max-t    nil | n                               ; greatest writer t folded; nil\n   618\t;;                                                          until one is (§4.2)\n   619\t;;    :rejected n                                     ; monotonic count of rejected\n   620\t;;                                                          batches — never drained (§2.2)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
