.md:242:1. **One `dao.jing.file` content handle**, made network-accessible with\ndocs/dao.space.stigmergy.md:243:   `dao.jing.remote/default-handlers`. A remote reader uses\ndocs/dao.space.stigmergy.md:244:   `dao.jing.remote/connect-content!`; both handles expose the same plain-data content\ndocs/dao.space.stigmergy.md:253:   materializes its opaque payloads into `dao.jing`. Readers query exactly the\ndocs/ideas/sqllite.md:13:`Yin.VM` relative to `dao.space`, `dao.space.query`, and `dao.jing`?\ndocs/ideas/sqllite.md:15:Laid side by side: `dao.jing` maps to SQLite's pager (a dumb, opaque byte store).\ndocs/ideas/sqllite.md:75:worth being precise that `dao.jing` itself stores opaque bytes, not a B-tree; the tree\ndocs/ideas/sqllite.md:77:bytes (`dao.jing` \"storage never interprets\"), so these primitives read as `dao.space.query`\ndocs/ideas/sqllite.md:79:`dao.jing`.\ndocs/ideas/sqllite.md:83:`dao.space.query` and `dao.jing` are a logical boundary, not a physical one:\ndocs/ideas/sqllite.md:84:`dao.jing.dht` is a content-handle backend that puts real network distance between a peer\ndocs/ideas/sqllite.md:89:`dao.jing.dht.md` states the intent outright: \"the engine will simply ask `dao.jing`\ndocs/ideas/sqllite.md:111:by content hash) and ship it, via `dao.stream.apply`, to the node where `dao.jing`\ndocs/ideas/sqllite.md:139:built, and `dao.jing.dht`'s own network backend is a partial first cut\ndocs/ideas/sqllite.md:140:(`dao.jing.dht.md`, Status table). The one thing that is solid across all of it: the\ndocs/ideas/sqllite.md:141:layering holds together without contradiction, `dao.jing` stays dumb, `dao.space.query`\ndocs/ideas/sqllite.md:154:- `docs/design/dao.jing.md` — the content-addressed storage observer\ndocs/ideas/sqllite.md:155:- `docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md` — the distributed content-handle backend\ndocs/agy-test.md:107:| **7** | `dao.jing.dht` & `dao.stream.ws` | Targeted Scenarios | Add DHT partition reconciliation tests & WS ping/pong keepalive tests. | **MEDIUM** |\ndocs/ideas/meerkat.md:6:**Status:** Synthesis note. Surfaces architectural decisions for `dao.jing`,\ndocs/ideas/meerkat.md:7:`dao.jing.dht`, and `dao.space`; does not change any code or API. The later DaoJing\ndocs/ideas/meerkat.md:31:That is `dao.jing`'s \"pure syntax, it holds form, never meaning... semantics an\ndocs/ideas/meerkat.md:32:interpreter projects onto it\" (`design/dao.jing.md`, *What DaoJing Is*). And\ndocs/ideas/meerkat.md:50:`design/dao.jing.dht.md`'s open section *`cas!` over the network*.\ndocs/ideas/meerkat.md:54:(`design/dao.jing.dht.md`, *`cas!`*). That is **register consensus**: agree on\ndocs/ideas/meerkat.md:83:`design/dao.jing.dht.md` (*`cas!` over the network*) proposes Algorand-style\ndocs/ideas/meerkat.md:101:`design/dao.jing.dht.md`'s own *Lineage* section already concedes the direction:\ndocs/ideas/meerkat.md:185:problems in `design/dao.jing.dht.md` are precisely about hiding, or making\ndocs/ideas/meerkat.md:218:(tuple-space) layer, not at `dao.jing`.\ndocs/ideas/meerkat.md:233:  which `design/dao.jing.dht.md` gestures at but does not solve.\ndocs/ideas/meerkat.md:240:  keeps operating with no increase in error-rate.\" `design/dao.jing.dht.md` lists\ndocs/ideas/meerkat.md:246:  intent for `dao.jing.dht` before the consensus code is written, not after.\ndocs/ideas/meerkat.md:284:   testing to the design intent for `dao.jing.dht` consensus code.\ndocs/ideas/meerkat.md:291:- `design/dao.jing.md` (the storage boundary; dumb-KV thesis; pure syntax)\ndocs/ideas/meerkat.md:292:- `design/dao.jing.dht.md` (`cas!` over the network; the five sortition open-questions; *Lineage*)\ndocs/ideas/mpi.md:6:**Terminology.** `dao.space` is the tuple space, the associative coordination surface agents match over; it is *not* the storage. Durable storage is `dao.jing`, the content-addressed store of opaque bytes built on `dao.stream`, the append-only log. When this document speaks of durability, immutability, replay, or history, it means `dao.jing` underneath `dao.space`; when it speaks of matching, coordination, and the collective vocabulary, it means `dao.space` itself. The protocol layer is `dao.flow`. The claim under review is really about all three together.\ndocs/ideas/mpi.md:47:4. **Auditability and Time-Travel**: MPI messages exist in flight and vanish. `dao.jing` preserves an immutable, queryable history of every claim, allocation, and decision. Resuming from a checkpoint is just replaying to a known cursor, with no special checkpoint infrastructure needed. \ndocs/ideas/mpi.md:56:   MPI is fundamentally transport-bound. `datom.world` scales from identical network semantics down to in-process sharing across heterogeneous runtimes using the same unified model. The \"zero-copy\" claim is scoped and worth stating precisely: what every runtime shares is the *`dao.jing` segment bytes* (immutable blobs each runtime reads lazily into its own datom representation, with no serialization round-trip for coordination state), not in-memory objects and not the bulk numerical payloads, which live in VRAM as handles outside `dao.space`. So this does not contest MPI's microsecond-kernel advantage (point 1 above, about moving raw buffers); it is a statement about coordination metadata spanning language boundaries cheaply, which MPI has no analog for at all.\ndocs/ideas/mpi.md:62:The claim is correct as stated for the **coordination model** and the **durability/decoupling advances**. `dao.space` + `dao.flow`, over `dao.jing` storage, is Linda's generative communication plus Datomic's durable history, fused: it recovers MPI's collective vocabulary in a more decoupled, replayable, dynamically-reconfigurable form, and adds associative addressing, emergent topology, the interpreter family, shared in-process storage across languages, and governed-computation security that MPI structurally lacks.\ndocs/jolt-experiment.md:38:| 230 | `No dependency provides java.security.MessageDigest` (jolt RFC 0014 `:jolt/provides` needed) | dao.jing, dao.jing.file, dao.jing.dht, dao.space.* (content addressing) |\ndocs/jolt-experiment.md:41:| 29 | `No matching field or method: Double/isFinite` | dao.jing, dao.postgraphics.math |\ndocs/jolt-experiment.md:42:| 25 | `No matching field or method: java.util.Arrays/parallelSort` | dao.stream.log → dao.jing.file fixture path |\ndocs/jolt-experiment.md:43:| 25 | `No matching ctor found for java.io.RandomAccessFile` | dao.jing.file |\ndocs/jolt-experiment.md:98:| MessageDigest (230) | one call in `dao.jing`, already reader-conditional per host | No |\ndocs/jolt-experiment.md:104:| DatagramSocket (13) | `dao.stream.udp` and `dao.jing.dht.node` | Half: dht.node duplicates the socket code |\ndocs/jolt-experiment.md:108:Two abstraction leaks: `dao.jing.dht.node` reimplements sockets instead of opening a udp stream, and `dao.jing.file` reaches into the log stream record's `:raf` field to fsync.\ndocs/jolt-experiment.md:131:The v2 plan explicitly defers file, UDP, log, relation and RPC transports and every consumer migration. `dao.jing`, `dao.jing.file`, `dao.jing.dht`, `dao.space` and `dao.runtime` (v1) still require legacy `dao.stream`, so the largest jolt error groups sit outside v2:\ndocs/jolt-experiment.md:133:- MessageDigest (230) is one call in `dao.jing`, unrelated to any stream version.\ndocs/jolt-experiment.md:134:- RandomAccessFile (25) is v1's log stream, which `dao.jing.file` also reaches into directly.\ndocs/jolt-experiment.md:135:- DatagramSocket (13) is v1's udp stream, duplicated inside `dao.jing.dht.node`.\ndocs/agents/advanced-concepts.md:2:description: Advanced concepts - parallel transport across dao.jing instances, capability tokens, entanglement/consensus\ndocs/agents/advanced-concepts.md:7:Parallel transport moves datoms from one dao.jing to another.\ndocs/agents/advanced-concepts.md:27:  It is NOT dao.jing/content-hash. That one is the *syntactic* hash — it addresses\ndocs/agents/advanced-concepts.md:56:  AST references resolve via th