{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"unzip -p /Users/sto/.m2/repository/org/replikativ/boring/0.1.30/boring-0.1.30.jar boring/options.cljc | rg -n 'defn resolve-opts|defn check-profile-conflicts|profile-locked|incompatible-options' -A75 -B15; unzip -p /Users/sto/.m2/repository/org/replikativ/boring/0.1.30/boring-0.1.30.jar org/replikativ/boring/core.clj | rg -n '\"'^'\"\\\\(defn encode|ONE CBOR|encode-indexed|index' -A35 -B10\"","aggregated_output":"67-\n68-;; Keys a profile DEFINES. Passing a conflicting value asks for two\n69-;; incompatible things at once, and the previous behaviour was to silently\n70-;; honour the user's -- so `{:profile :canonical :stringref true}` emitted the\n71-;; stringref extension from the profile whose entire purpose is agreement with\n72-;; other implementations, and `{:profile :canonical :canonical false}` turned\n73-;; determinism off while still calling itself canonical.\n74-;;\n75-;; `:canonical` is locked in EVERY profile: it is not a user knob, it is what\n76-;; `:profile :canonical` MEANS. Accepting it separately gave two ways to say\n77-;; the same thing that could disagree.\n78-;;\n79-;; `:stringref` stays free under :clojure -- kabel legitimately turns it off\n80-;; while keeping the default profile -- and `:float-policy` stays free under\n81-;; :interop, where either width is legal CBOR that every reader understands.\n82:(def profile-locked\n83-  {:clojure           #{:canonical :canonical-order}\n84-   :interop           #{:canonical :canonical-order :stringref :shapes}\n85-   ;; Everything is locked, as under :canonical: both bits are what the profile\n86-   ;; MEANS. `:float-policy :shortest` here would just be :canonical spelled\n87-   ;; oddly, and `:canonical false` would just be :interop -- two more ways to\n88-   ;; say things that already have names.\n89-   :archival          #{:canonical :canonical-order :stringref :shapes :float-policy}\n90-   :canonical         #{:canonical :canonical-order :stringref :shapes :float-policy}\n91-   :canonical-rfc7049 #{:canonical :canonical-order :stringref :shapes :float-policy}})\n92-\n93-;; RESOLUTION IS IDEMPOTENT, and it is marked rather than inferred.\n94-;;\n95-;; It was not, and that cost two separate breakages in one afternoon -- 415\n96-;; test errors from `nav/source` and 6 more from `seal-index!`, both from the\n97-;; same cause. A resolved map carries the profile's own `:canonical`, so\n98-;; re-resolving reads that as the caller trying to override what the profile\n99:;; locks, and the second resolve throws `:boring/incompatible-options` on a map\n100-;; the first resolve produced.\n101-;;\n102-;; That is a trap for every caller that passes options down: whether you may\n103-;; resolve depends on whether somebody above you already did, which is not\n104-;; knowable locally. The marker makes it knowable. Metadata because it is\n105-;; inert -- nothing reads the options map except by keyword, and it is never\n106-;; encoded.\n107-(def ^:private resolved-meta {::resolved true})\n108-\n109-(defn resolved?\n110-  \"Whether `opts` has already been through `resolve-opts`.\"\n111-  [opts]\n112-  (boolean (::resolved (meta opts))))\n113-\n114-;; The resolved options for `(encode v)` / `(decode bs)`, computed once.\n115-;; nil opts is the dominant call shape and always resolves to the same map.\n116-(def default-opts (with-meta (:clojure profile-defaults) resolved-meta))\n117-\n118-;; ------------------------------------------------------------- the placeholder\n119-\n120-(defn unencodable\n121-  \"The default `:encode-fallback` placeholder: a tag-27 frame naming the type\n122-  that could not be encoded, with its `pr-str`.\n123-\n124-  Readable by any CBOR implementation, and obviously a placeholder rather than\n125-  a value that might be mistaken for the original.\"\n126-  [x]\n127-  (data/unknown-record \"boring/unencodable\"\n128-                       {:type #?(:clj (.getName (class x))\n129-                                 ;; `(str (type x))` is the constructor's\n130-                                 ;; COMPILED SOURCE on ClojureScript -- for\n131-                                 ;; `(atom 1)` it is\n132-                                 ;; `function He(){this.state=1;...}`. Unbounded,\n133-                                 ;; different in every build, and it leaks\n134-                                 ;; implementation into a placeholder that gets\n135-                                 ;; WRITTEN TO STORAGE. It also defeats\n136-                                 ;; `:archival`, whose whole point is that two\n137-                                 ;; dumps of the same value compare equal.\n138-                                 ;;\n139-                                 ;; The constructor's `.name` is minified under\n140-                                 ;; `:advanced` and so is not stable across\n141-                                 ;; builds either -- but it is SHORT and it does\n142-                                 ;; not embed source. There is no stable type\n143-                                 ;; name on this platform; the placeholder says\n144-                                 ;; what it can rather than pasting a function\n145-                                 ;; body into the wire.\n146-                                 :cljs (let [t (type x)\n147-                                             n (some-> t .-name)]\n148-                                         (if (and n (seq n)) n \"unknown\")))\n149-                        :repr (pr-str x)}))\n150-\n151-(defn fallback-fn\n152-  \"`:encode-fallback` as the writer wants it: nil (throw, the default), or a\n153-  function of the offending value returning a replacement.\n154-\n155-  The only coercion in this namespace, and it is here rather than in the\n156-  writers because both of them needed it and `:placeholder` has to mean the\n157-  same function on both platforms.\"\n158-  [fb]\n159-  (if (= :placeholder fb) unencodable fb))\n160-\n161-;; ------------------------------------------------------------- the predicates\n162-;;\n163-;; Few and named, deliberately. These are the only places the two platforms can\n164-;; legitimately differ, so a divergence has to be introduced HERE, in a\n165-;; four-line function with a name, rather than inlined into a table row.\n166-\n167-;; The decoder recurses per level, so a limit the host stack cannot honour is\n168-;; not a limit: `:max-depth 4000` accepted a 2000-deep document by raising a\n169-;; raw StackOverflowError. 2048 is the most that has been measured to hold on a\n170-;; default stack; the default of 1024 is well inside it.\n171-(def ^:const max-safe-depth 2048)\n172-\n173-;; The wire format's slot arithmetic is 32-bit on both platforms, so this is\n174-;; the ceiling on both -- it is a property of the FORMAT, not of the host's\n--\n207-;; ------------------------------------------------------------------ the spec\n208-;;\n209-;; One row per option, and the row is the whole rule: what a legal value is,\n210-;; and what to say when it is not. `:pred` is called only when the key is\n211-;; PRESENT, so a default never has to satisfy its own predicate at runtime.\n212-;;\n213-;; WHICH LEAVES THE DEFAULTS UNGUARDED, and this comment used to end \"-- the\n214-;; test freezes the defaults instead\", naming a test that does not exist.\n215-;; Nothing under `test/` requires `boring.options` at all: `option_matrix.cljc`\n216-;; freezes VERDICTS (what each entry point does with a given map) and\n217-;; `generative_test.cljc` freezes the profile NAMES, both through\n218-;; `boring.core`. A default widened here -- `:max-depth` to 100 000, say -- is\n219-;; caught only if some behavioural assertion happens to notice.\n220-;;\n221-;; The same gap covers this namespace's whole exported surface. `spec`,\n222:;; `profile-defaults`, `profile-locked`, `max-safe-depth`, `max-index-stride`,\n223-;; `validate!` and `check-profile-conflicts!` have no reference anywhere in\n224-;; `src/`, `test/` or `bench/` outside this file, so every one of them is\n225-;; reachable only through the paths `resolve-opts` and `check-opts` happen to\n226-;; take. That is fine for the code and not fine for the guarantee.\n227-\n228-(def spec\n229-  {:profile        {:pred #(contains? profile-defaults %)\n230-                    :type :boring/unknown-profile\n231-                    :want \"one of :clojure, :interop, :archival, :canonical, :canonical-rfc7049\"}\n232-   :max-depth      {:pred #(int-in? % 1 max-safe-depth)\n233-                    :want (str \"a positive integer no greater than \" max-safe-depth\n234-                               \" (the decoder recurses per level, and a limit the host \"\n235-                               \"stack cannot honour is not a limit)\")}\n236-   :max-items      {:pred #(int-in? % 0 #?(:clj Long/MAX_VALUE :cljs js/Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER))\n237-                    :want \"a non-negative integer (0 means unlimited)\"}\n238-   :chunk-size     {:pred #(int-in? % 1 max-index-stride)\n239-                    :want \"a positive integer\"}\n240-   :index          {:pred #(int-in? % 0 max-index-stride)\n241-                    :want (str \"a non-negative integer no greater than \" max-index-stride\n242-                               \" (0 turns indexing off)\")}\n243-   :index-min      {:pred #(int-in? % 0 max-index-stride)\n244-                    :want (str \"a non-negative integer no greater than \" max-index-stride)}\n245-   :float-policy   {:pred #{:preserve-width :shortest}\n246-                    :want \":preserve-width or :shortest\"}\n247-   ;; `:canonical` and `:canonical-order` are LOCKED BY EVERY PROFILE, so\n248:   ;; passing either can only ever produce `:boring/incompatible-options`. They\n249-   ;; stay in the spec deliberately rather than being dropped: a dropped key is\n250-   ;; an unknown key, and `{:cannonical true}` would then be indistinguishable\n251-   ;; from a stray one. The conflict error names the profile and the value it\n252-   ;; defines, which is more use than silence. Say what you want by NAMING a\n253-   ;; profile -- that is what a profile is.\n254-   :canonical      {:pred boolish? :want \"true or false\"}\n255-   :canonical-order {:pred #{:rfc8949 :rfc7049}\n256-                     :want \":rfc8949 or :rfc7049\"}\n257-   ;; A function is legal on both platforms: ClojureScript has one time type,\n258-   ;; so a caller wanting `cljc.java-time`/`tick` values supplies the\n259-   ;; constructor rather than boring depending on js-joda. `(fn [epoch-millis])`.\n260-   :instant-type   {:pred #(or (#{:date :instant} %) (fn? %))\n261-                    :want \":date, :instant, or a function of epoch millis\"}\n262-   :date-type      {:pred #{:local-date :sql-date}\n263-                    :want \":local-date or :sql-date\"}\n264-   :encode-fallback {:pred #(or (nil? %) (= :placeholder %) (callable? %))\n265-                     :want \"nil, :placeholder, or a function\"}\n266-   ;; What an unregistered tag-27 name decodes to.\n267-   ;;\n268-   ;;   :fallback  UnknownRecord / TaggedLiteral (the default -- lossless\n269-   ;;              passthrough, which is why the carrier exists)\n270-   ;;   :error     :boring/unregistered-record\n271-   ;;   a function of [name payload], whose return value is used\n272-   ;;\n273-   ;; `:error` exists because passthrough HIDES a registry that can never\n274-   ;; match. When a record's wire name became `namespace/Name`, a registration\n275-   ;; still keyed on the munged class name stopped matching and the records\n276-   ;; came back as UnknownRecord with no error -- caught here only by a suite\n277-   ;; that asserted equality with the input.\n278-   ;;\n279-   ;; No `:warn`: boring would have to own an output channel (`*err*` versus\n280-   ;; `js/console.warn`) and the dedupe state to keep a 200 000-item log from\n281-   ;; flooding, and neither is testable without capturing output. The function\n282-   ;; form is that capability without boring choosing a logger for anyone --\n283-   ;; `boring.data/frame-for` is public so a handler can warn and then return\n284-   ;; the default.\n285-   :on-unknown-record {:pred #(or (#{:fallback :error} %) (callable? %))\n286-                       :want \":fallback, :error, or a function of [name payload]\"}\n287-   ;; How much of a sealed index to believe, for `boring.nav`.\n288-   ;;\n289-   ;;   :trusted  use it (the default, and today's behaviour)\n290-   ;;   :ignore   never read it -- always scan\n291-   ;;\n292-   ;; `:ignore` exists because a chosen index can misdirect a lookup WITHIN the\n293-   ;; blob it came with. That gains an attacker nothing directly -- they wrote\n294-   ;; every byte, so they could have sent the value they misdirect you to. It\n295-   ;; matters when an application verifies one part of a document and then acts\n296-   ;; on another: two `get`s can be made to resolve to overlapping regions, so\n297-   ;; you checked one thing and used a different one. Scanning removes the\n298-   ;; question, at the cost of the acceleration.\n299-   ;;\n300-   ;; A third value, `:validate` -- walk every anchor chain at load and refuse\n301-   ;; the index if any link fails -- is measured (about one unindexed scan:\n302-   ;; 12.2 ms on a 4.6 MB, 200 000-item file) and not yet implemented. It is\n303-   ;; deliberately NOT accepted here until it is, because an option that names\n304-   ;; a behaviour it does not perform is worse than one that does not exist.\n305-   :trust-index    {:pred #{:trusted :ignore}\n306-                    :want \":trusted or :ignore\"}\n307-   :registry       {:pred #(or (nil? %) (registry? %))\n308-                    :want #?(:clj \"a TagRegistry from boring/tag-registry\"\n309-                             :cljs \"a registry map from boring/tag-registry\")}\n310-   ;; The booleans. `(boolean v)` accepts anything, which is the same shape of\n311-   ;; silence `:float-policy` had: `{:validate-utf8 \"false\"}` read as TRUE and\n312-   ;; turned nothing off, on the option doc/SECURITY.md names for lenient\n313-   ;; decoding.\n314-   :stringref                    {:pred boolish? :want \"true or false\"}\n315-   :shapes                       {:pred boolish? :want \"true or false\"}\n316-   :incl-metadata?               {:pred boolish? :want \"true or false\"}\n317-   :permit-reserved-simple-values {:pred boolish? :want \"true or false\"}\n318-   :tolerate-unknown-tags        {:pred boolish? :want \"true or false\"}\n319-   :validate-utf8                {:pred boolish? :want \"true or false\"}\n320-   :check-duplicate-keys         {:pred boolish? :want \"true or false\"}\n321-   :auto-construct-records?      {:pred boolish? :want \"true or false\"}})\n322-\n323-(defn- bad-option! [k v want type]\n--\n386-                                     {:type :boring/bad-option :option k\n387-                                      :did-you-mean m})))))\n388-               nil)\n389-             nil opts)\n390-  opts)\n391-\n392-;; ------------------------------------------------------------------- resolve\n393-\n394-;; Allocation-free unless there IS a conflict.\n395-;;\n396-;; This runs on EVERY encode and decode that passes options. The first version\n397-;; built a sorted set through `into` whatever the input, so the common call\n398-;; paid for a reduction and a set allocation to discover it could not conflict\n399-;; with anything. The ClojureScript copy kept that version for months after the\n400-;; JVM one was fixed, which is the whole argument for this file.\n401:(defn check-profile-conflicts! [profile base opts]\n402-  (let [conflicts (when (seq opts)\n403-                    (reduce (fn [acc k]\n404-                              (if (and (contains? opts k)\n405-                                       (not= (get opts k) (get base k)))\n406-                                (conj (or acc []) k)\n407-                                acc))\n408-                            nil\n409:                            (profile-locked profile)))]\n410-    (when (seq conflicts)\n411-      (throw (ex-info (str \"boring: \" (pr-str (vec conflicts))\n412-                           \" cannot be overridden under the \" profile\n413-                           \" profile -- the profile defines \"\n414-                           (pr-str (select-keys base conflicts)))\n415:                      {:type :boring/incompatible-options\n416-                       :profile profile\n417-                       :conflicts (vec conflicts)\n418-                       :profile-values (select-keys base conflicts)})))))\n419-\n420-(defn- profile-of [opts]\n421-  (let [profile (get opts :profile :clojure)]\n422-    (or (profile-defaults profile)\n423-        (bad-option! :profile profile (:want (:profile spec))\n424-                     :boring/unknown-profile))))\n425-\n426-(defn check-opts\n427-  \"The gate for the DECODE side: validate, and return `opts` untouched.\n428-\n429-  Not `resolve-opts`, because no profile key reaches a Reader -- a profile\n430-  names `:stringref`, `:float-policy`, `:canonical`, `:canonical-order` and\n431-  `:shapes`, and `configure-reader!` reads none of them. Merging would allocate\n432-  a fresh map of ~230-300 bytes on every `decode` call that passes options, to\n433-  add five keys nothing downstream looks at.\n434-\n435-  The profile is still LOOKED UP, so `{:profile :nope}` is refused here as it\n436-  is on the encode side. That asymmetry -- `decode` and `decode-seq` accepting\n437-  an unknown profile while `decode-with` and `decode-seq-from` refused it --\n438-  was one of the six measured disagreements this namespace exists to end.\"\n439-  [opts]\n440-  (when (some? opts)\n441-    ;; The profile is only looked up when one was NAMED. `resolve-opts` needs\n442-    ;; the profile's map and pays for the lookup either way; this side only\n443-    ;; needs to know the name is real, and most decode calls do not pass one.\n444-    (when (contains? opts :profile) (profile-of opts))\n445-    (validate! opts))\n446-  opts)\n447-\n448:(defn resolve-opts\n449-  \"The one gate. Validate, apply the profile, merge.\n450-\n451-  Every public entry point on both platforms calls exactly this and passes the\n452-  result down; nothing downstream re-validates. `nil` short-circuits to the\n453-  frozen default map without touching any of it, which is the dominant call\n454-  shape and the reason the validation above is affordable.\"\n455-  [opts]\n456-  (cond\n457-    (nil? opts) default-opts\n458-    ;; Already through the gate -- see `resolved?`. Returning it unchanged is\n459-    ;; what lets a function resolve its own argument without having to know\n460-    ;; whether its caller did.\n461-    (resolved? opts) opts\n462-    :else (let [base (profile-of opts)]\n463-            (validate! opts)\n464-            (check-profile-conflicts! (get opts :profile :clojure) base opts)\n465-            (with-meta (merge base (dissoc opts :profile)) resolved-meta))))\ncaution: filename not matched:  org/replikativ/boring/core.clj\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
